WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.251
Moving day, and a different person for each kind of job.

2
00:00:06.251 --> 00:00:09.991
A helper with a clipboard writes new cards from the labels.

3
00:00:09.991 --> 00:00:12.211
A mover opens a box and repacks it.

4
00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:16.142
Someone at the new house unpacks a box to check what's inside.

5
00:00:16.142 --> 00:00:19.565
And an older helper reads only labels written in Java.

6
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.231
Eight lessons were about the helper with the clipboard.

7
00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:32.303
Lesson 1 gave him two limits, in the docs' own words: processors can't examine expressions or statements, and they can't modify the source code.

8
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:40.259
Now say Java callers want lamp.inventoryCard() as a real member function, inside Lamp itself.

9
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:44.333
Here's the workaround many of us think of, and never say out loud.

10
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:50.251
If the helper can't add a function to Lamp, can't he just write Lamp again, with the function inside?

11
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:51.727
Let's try it.

12
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:54.862
RepackDemo's processor does exactly that.

13
00:00:54.862 --> 00:01:03.425
It finds Lamp, and writes a new file, LampExtra.kt, with a second data class Lamp that has inventoryCard inside.

14
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.795
KSP lets it write the file.

15
00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:07.236
What happens next?

16
00:01:07.236 --> 00:01:08.517
Pause and guess.

17
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.721
The Kotlin compiler meets two classes with one name.

18
00:01:15.721 --> 00:01:18.737
Redeclaration, in LampExtra.kt, line 3.

19
00:01:18.737 --> 00:01:21.676
And Redeclaration, in Lamp.kt, line 6.

20
00:01:21.676 --> 00:01:26.084
It points at both boxes, and refuses to pick a favourite.

21
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.013
KSP never checks what's inside a new file.

22
00:01:29.013 --> 00:01:34.825
It hands the file to the compiler along with yours, and the compiler won't keep two Lamps.

23
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:42.193
That's a helper who can't open a box, so he packs a second box with the same label and hopes nobody notices.

24
00:01:42.193 --> 00:01:44.049
The compiler always notices.

25
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:45.894
Now the way that works.

26
00:01:45.894 --> 00:01:52.970
The second compile writes a new card next to the box: an extension function, fun Lamp.inventoryCard.

27
00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.428
It compiles, and desk.inventoryCard() returns the card we know.

28
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:01.667
In Kotlin it reads like a member, but Lamp is untouched.

29
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:08.151
A Java caller writes LampExtraKt.inventoryCard(desk): a static method that takes the lamp.

30
00:02:08.151 --> 00:02:15.211
Java's annotation processors have the same limit, so the Java twin is a separate class, LampCards.

31
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.994
So some jobs need the class itself to change.

32
00:02:20.994 --> 00:02:27.426
That's a compiler plugin: a jar the Kotlin compiler loads to change what it writes into class files.

33
00:02:27.426 --> 00:02:30.707
In the picture, it's the mover who repacks the box.

34
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:33.396
The Advanced Kotlin track met three.

35
00:02:33.396 --> 00:02:37.637
all-open removes final from classes a framework must subclass.

36
00:02:37.637 --> 00:02:41.262
no-arg adds a constructor that Kotlin code can't see.

37
00:02:41.262 --> 00:02:46.734
kotlinx.serialization writes a serializer inside each class marked Serializable.

38
00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:52.001
No processor can do any of those, because each one changes a class you wrote.

39
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:53.756
Good news and bad news.

40
00:02:53.756 --> 00:02:56.734
The good news: the mover can repack anything.

41
00:02:56.734 --> 00:03:01.169
The bad news: the box you packed is not quite the box that arrives.

42
00:03:01.169 --> 00:03:06.596
The class you read isn't the class the JVM loads, so a plugin asks for more trust.

43
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.800
Libraries show the same line.

44
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:21.539
Checked in November 2026: Arrow Optics now recommends its compiler plugin, because KSP can add functions to a companion object but can't create one.

45
00:03:21.539 --> 00:03:30.581
Koin deprecated its KSP compiler for the Koin Compiler Plugin, and Kotlin DataFrame deprecated its KSP plugin too.

46
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:32.800
So here's the rule.

47
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.044
If the job is to change a class, it's a compiler plugin's job.

48
00:03:37.044 --> 00:03:41.013
If it's to write a new file next to the class, it's KSP's.

49
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.609
Now the whole choice, in three questions.

50
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:47.557
First: must the job change a class you wrote?

51
00:03:47.557 --> 00:03:50.159
Then it needs a compiler plugin, the mover.

52
00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:53.572
If not: does it run a few times, on a few classes?

53
00:03:53.572 --> 00:03:56.906
Then reflection is fine: unpacking at the new house.

54
00:03:56.906 --> 00:04:02.228
Earlier in this lesson, a warm reflection card cost about a fifth of a microsecond.

55
00:04:02.228 --> 00:04:05.497
Nobody waits for that, unless it's a million cards.

56
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.286
If not: does a KSP processor exist for the job?

57
00:04:09.286 --> 00:04:12.796
Then use KSP, the helper who writes new cards.

58
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.813
And if only a Java processor exists, kapt runs it: the older helper.

59
00:04:17.813 --> 00:04:23.829
Every label is first copied out in Java, as stubs, and that copying is the slow part.

60
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.735
So, can the helper write the class again?

61
00:04:27.735 --> 00:04:30.820
No: two Lamps, and the compiler stops the build.

62
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.583
A helper adds new files next to your boxes.

63
00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:36.668
When the box itself must change, call the mover.

64
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.794
Quick check.

65
00:04:37.794 --> 00:04:41.297
A framework needs your classes open, not final.

66
00:04:41.297 --> 00:04:43.533
Could a KSP processor do that?

67
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:46.919
No.

68
00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.981
Opening a class changes a class you wrote.

69
00:04:49.981 --> 00:04:52.971
That's a compiler plugin's job: all-open.
